    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Description\n\nFirstly, let me say thank you for all your work on these awesome mini.nvim plugins! ❤️❤️❤️\r\n\r\nI was hoping you [might reconsider](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/397#issuecomment-1615999605) preserving html attributes when replacing tag via `srtt`. Here are a few reasons why I think it's in scope of your plugin to do so 🙏\r\n\r\n1. Often when changing a tag in HTML, it's because the user renamed a JS component, or because they want to switch to a different header level (ie. `<h2>` to `<h3>`), etc. In both scenarios, they would want to keep your HTML classes or JS component props, despite the tag rename.\r\n\r\n2. Users coming over from [tpope/vim-surround](https://github.com/tpope/vim-surround) are used to this functionality, as vim-surround preserves HTML attributes when replacing tags. This would make the move to mini.surround more enticing for vim-surround users.\r\n\r\n3. You mention dealing with HTML attributes is out of scope, but if the user adds surrounding tags via `sat` and types `h3 class=\"font-bold\"`, I notice you already nicely handle adding that attribute to the opening tag, but not to the closing tag (ie. `<h3 class=\"font-bold\">...</h3>`). Given that you're already handling attributes so nicely with `sa`, why not also with `sr`?\r\n\r\n4. I can't think of any downsides to preserving HTML attributes with `sr`. I personally LOVE mini.surround, but really miss attribute preservation in tpope's plugin, and find myself without a solution for this. If some people rely on the current functionality, maybe it could be a configuration option that we could opt-in to or opt-out of?\r\n\r\nYou've created a wonderful plugin, and I want to thank you again for your work on it! Curious what you think of the above? 🙏",
