            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the detailed reasons.\r\n\r\nAll motivation for the current behavior is outlined in the linked comment.\r\n\r\n> 3\\. You mention dealing with HTML attributes is out of scope ...\r\n\r\nNot HTML attributes, but specifically preserving attributes during \"replace\" action. The reason for that is described in the linked comment: during `srtt` the first `t` matches the whole left and right `<...>` (as it is its \"input\" specification) and the second `t` replaces them with content supplied by the user (as it is its \"output\" specification).\r\n\r\nReplacing \"input\" specification in `t` surrounding to mean only \"tag name inside <...>\" would lead to `sdt` deleting only that part and not the whole `<...>` left and right parts. Which is probably not what most users expect.\r\n\r\n> 4\\. I can't think of any downsides to preserving HTML attributes with `sr`.\r\n\r\nThe main downside is that it doesn't fit the design of surrounding specification in 'mini.surround' and how \"replace\" action works in general.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nOne way of doing so (which should have come to mind sooner) is to create a separate surrounding specifically for \"tag name\". With something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.surround').setup({\r\n  custom_surroundings = {\r\n    T = {\r\n      input = { '<(%w+)[^<>]->.-</%1>', '^<()%w+().*</()%w+()>$' },\r\n      output = function()\r\n        local tag_name = MiniSurround.user_input('Tag name')\r\n        if tag_name == nil then return nil end\r\n        return { left = tag_name, right = tag_name }\r\n      end,\r\n    },\r\n  },\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow replacing a tag name can be done with `srTT`, replacing the whole tag pair with another tag pair with `srtt`, and deleting tag pair is also the same with `sdt`.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned, duplicate, and which have a workaround.",
