        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> [@echasnovski](https://github.com/echasnovski) Can't at least `T` be part of the package?\n\nI don't think it is a good idea to have this built-in for 'mini.surround' itself. The `t` surrounding itself is only present because `it` and `at` are built-in textobjects and 'mini.surround' + 'mini.ai' are kept as aligned as reasonably possible. Otherwise it would not have been built-in because it is not very versatile for any non-add action.\n\nMaybe if there are some language packs in the future, then including this dedicated surrounding for languages that use a lot of HTML tags might make sense.\n\n> Correction: The issue is not about the arrow function but hyphens in the name.\n> \n> even this\n> \n> <my-element>test</my-element>\n> \n> will fail to rename\n\nYes, because the example only assumes alphanumeric characters as part of a tag name (same as default `t` surrounding and textobject from 'mini.ai'). To account for that, you can try to use `[%w_-]` instead of every `%w` in the `input` pattern.\n\nAnd as always with anything tag surrounding/textobject related, my suggestion is still to try and use tree-sitter here. It might be a bit tougher for this \"tag name\" surrounding, but should be possible.",
